**Image formats.** A *raw* image (often made with `dd`) is the block row byte for byte, nothing more. The **E01** format, properly EWF (Expert Witness Compression Format, originally specified by ASR Data and used by EnCase and FTK), wraps the same data with extras ([libewf format notes](https://github.com/libyal/libewf/blob/main/documentation/Expert%20Witness%20Compression%20Format%20%28EWF%29.asciidoc)):

- the image can be split across *segment files* (`.E01`, `.E02` and so on);
- data is stored in chunks (by default 64 sectors of 512 bytes), optionally compressed;
- header sections record case details such as case number and examiner name;
- a hash section stores the MD5 of the acquired media.

Tools such as The Sleuth Kit and Autopsy read both raw and E01 images, so the choice is about storage size and built-in metadata, not about what can be examined.

Inside an inode ([kernel: inodes](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/ext4/inodes.html)):

- offsets 0x08 `atime`, 0x0C `ctime` (inode change), 0x10 `mtime`, 0x14 `dtime` (deletion time), each seconds since 1970 UTC;
- by default ext4 inodes are 256 bytes, which adds `crtime` (creation, offset 0x90) and `_extra` fields: two bits extend the seconds so times do not overflow until May 2446, thirty bits give nanoseconds;
- ext2 and ext3 used fixed 128-byte inodes, which have none of that: no creation time, whole seconds only;
- `crtime` and `dtime` are not visible through `stat()`; debugfs (the ext2/ext3/ext4 file system debugger) reports them;
- `i_block` at offset 0x28 starts with an extent header, magic 0xF30A ([extent tree](https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/ext4/ifork.html)), which lists the file's block ranges.

*windows-for-security* showed the registry as configuration and a persistence spot (Run keys). On a disk image it is also a record of what the machine and its users did. Microsoft defines a **hive** as a group of keys, subkeys and values with supporting files that are loaded when the system starts or a user logs on ([Registry Hives](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/sysinfo/registry-hives)):

- `HKLM\SAM`, `SECURITY`, `SOFTWARE` and `SYSTEM` come from files of those names in `%SystemRoot%\System32\Config`;
- each user's `HKEY_CURRENT_USER` comes from `NTUSER.DAT` in their profile, and a second user hive, `UsrClass.dat`, sits under `AppData\Local\Microsoft\Windows`;
- `.log` files beside a hive are transaction logs of changes to it. Collect them with the hive: recent changes may be there and not yet in the main file.

Every forensic tool is a parser fed with data the suspect controlled. If the parser has bugs, the evidence can attack the examiner. *memory-safety-bugs* showed how crafted input exploits a parser; here the parser is the forensic suite.

**The case.** Cellebrite sells UFED, which extracts data from phones, and Physical Analyzer, which parses the extraction for browsing. On 21 April 2021 Signal published an analysis of both ([Signal](https://signal.org/blog/cellebrite-vulnerabilities/)). It reported:

- the FFmpeg libraries (an open-source media framework) bundled for media parsing had been built in 2012 and not updated since, despite over a hundred security updates to FFmpeg in that time;
- industry-standard exploit mitigations were missing;
- so a specially formatted file inside any app on a scanned phone could run code on the examiner's Cellebrite machine and alter its reports, past and future, with *no detectable timestamp changes or checksum failures*;
- the installer also bundled Apple-signed DLLs (shared libraries), which Signal suggested Apple might not have licensed.

**Validation is the discipline that answers this.** NIST's Computer Forensics Tool Testing (CFTT) programme, supported by the US Department of Homeland Security, publishes tool specifications, test procedures and test reports for categories including disk imaging, write blockers, deleted file recovery, file carving, mobile devices, Windows registry tools and SQLite ([NIST CFTT](https://www.nist.gov/itl/ssd/software-quality-group/computer-forensics-tool-testing-program-cftt)). SP 800-86 already pointed to CFTT for imaging tools in 2006.

In your own lab:

- validate each tool version against reference images with known answers (NIST's CFReDS portal publishes such data sets; [CFReDS](https://cfreds.nist.gov/));
- confirm key findings with a second, independent tool, and against the raw bytes;
- keep the analysis workstation isolated and rebuilt from a known state;
- record tool names and versions in your notes and report.
